  [38;5;153mUSER_HASH_SALT[39m is also in the bundle, which is the limitation I described in Section 2.

  The real cost to call out: [1mopening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly from the filesystem stops working.[0m Today that's how this app
  runs. After this, you need [38;5;153mnpm install && npm run build[39m first. That's a genuine workflow regression for a 15-line
  page, and it's the price of readable production stack traces — the thing you said you need.

  One thing to decide, since this repo has no tooling at all and a build step is the cheapest moment it will ever be to
  add some. Each of these is far more expensive to retrofit later, once there are more files and more call sites to
  bring into compliance:

  - [1mLint + format (eslint + prettier)[0m — catches the class of bug that produces your production errors in the first
    place, and settles formatting arguments permanently. Near-zero ongoing cost once configured.
  - [1mUnit tests (vitest)[0m — the redaction allowlist is exactly the code that must not silently regress: a test that
    asserts a password-shaped field never survives [38;5;153mredact()[39m is the safety net for the whole privacy design. I'd call
    this one close to mandatory given what we just designed.
  - [1mEnd-to-end test (Playwright)[0m — drives the real form in a real browser and asserts the right events were emitted.
    Highest confidence, highest setup cost, and the most infrastructure for a page this size.

  My recommendation is lint+format and unit tests, and to skip end-to-end for now — the unit tests cover the part where
  a mistake has privacy consequences, while e2e mostly re-verifies that a form submit fires, which is cheap to check by
  hand at this size.

